{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"4cb985213cddcb76","type":"group","x":-1180,"y":4720,"width":4380,"height":1800,"label":"Localized Model"},
		{"id":"0b35541ce2349283","type":"group","x":-1030,"y":620,"width":3650,"height":960,"label":"Biology"},
		{"id":"64d5dc0a7b700d9e","type":"group","x":1150,"y":2455,"width":2540,"height":1265,"label":"Psychology"},
		{"id":"b30688769aa717a8","type":"group","x":-380,"y":7000,"width":3580,"height":880,"label":"Connectionist Model"},
		{"id":"beb8a5c9b421e7eb","type":"group","x":1010,"y":4800,"width":1305,"height":1680,"label":"Modern Neuroscience started from Language Disorders"},
		{"id":"e292ac944e20b7d1","type":"group","x":10,"y":-320,"width":1120,"height":880,"label":"Electrophysiology"},
		{"id":"61b0ad83d7d62391","type":"group","x":-1160,"y":4800,"width":369,"height":1680,"label":"Phrenology"},
		{"id":"ac6883ad975819a3","type":"group","x":-470,"y":-1040,"width":866,"height":600,"label":"Philosophy"},
		{"id":"866eeb4bcd79622c","type":"group","x":810,"y":1760,"width":400,"height":520,"label":"Behavorial Biology"},
		{"id":"a70bba50381456f9","type":"group","x":1330,"y":5320,"width":985,"height":97,"label":"Language Disorder Studies"},
		{"id":"4f44f3eea2b11535","type":"text","text":"American embryologist Ross Harrison\n- Tip of the axon gives rise to an expansion, the growth cone, which leads the developing axon to its target, either to other nerve cells or muscles.\n\n**-> Supporting the neuron doctrine theory**","x":1730,"y":920,"width":329,"height":291},
		{"id":"a3fdb8b04daa4ad7","type":"text","text":"- Spawned **cognitive neuroscience**, linking brain structures to specific mental processes.\n- Developed experimental paradigms still used in fMRI, EEG, and lesion studies.","x":3109,"y":3104,"width":561,"height":124},
		{"id":"05da82928f638b29","type":"text","text":"- Experimental **manipulation of brain chemistry**\n- Clarified the role of neurotransmitters (dopamine, serotonin) in mood, psychosis, and cognition.\n- Bridged psychology, psychiatry, and neurobiology.","x":3169,"y":3478,"width":501,"height":140},
		{"id":"6b5f97e1cb6a9c94","type":"text","text":"Originally started from a toy (I'm serious)![[stampfer-juggler-ani.gif]]","x":1770,"y":3282,"width":340,"height":378,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"ea154750e14083c8","type":"text","text":" - Influenced study of **visual perception**, pattern recognition, and sensory integration.\n - Laid conceptual groundwork for modern **cognitive neuroscience**.","x":2130,"y":3282,"width":329,"height":220},
		{"id":"0484f55128c6afdf","type":"text","text":"### Humanistic Psychology \n\nEmphasis on subjective experience, self-actualization, and motivation.\n\n-Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow","x":2450,"y":2720,"width":329,"height":200},
		{"id":"5a60b054f983f645","type":"text","text":"### Cognitive Psychology \n\nFocus on **mental processes** such as memory, attention, perception, and language.\n\n-Ulric Neisser, George Miller","x":2616,"y":3063,"width":329,"height":207},
		{"id":"1243b16351951d17","type":"text","text":"### Modern Psychiatry & Integration with Medicine\n\nClinical Psychology. Development of drugs to treat psychiatric disorders (antipsychotics, antidepressants, anxiolytics).\n\n--John Cade, Paul Janssen, Herbert Meltzer","x":2780,"y":3400,"width":329,"height":296},
		{"id":"b348b04524aa31d4","type":"text","text":"Pavlov's work on Classical Conditioning (Pavlov's Dog) gave way to understanding the neurochemical mechanisms behind learning & memory later by Skinner.","x":1410,"y":3098,"width":329,"height":184},
		{"id":"f0dd505007eb6655","type":"text","text":"### Pharmacological Advancements\n\n**Claude Bernard (France)** - Work on **drug actions and receptor-like specificity**.\n\n**Paul Ehrlich (Germany)** - Proposed the **“receptor theory”** around the **early 1900s** \n\n**John Newport Langley (England)** - Coined/advanced the idea of **“receptive substances”** ","x":1170,"y":3840,"width":329,"height":360},
		{"id":"1ca1fb7dcf7d3cdf","type":"text","text":"# Neuroscience didn't emerge from one discipline alone","x":-1870,"y":1585,"width":460,"height":95},
		{"id":"95bda17fe3ed4610","type":"text","text":" Cajal and Sherrington developed neuron doctrine and cellular connectionism.\n- Sherrington coined the term \"synapse\".","x":1170,"y":1380,"width":329,"height":180},
		{"id":"08f1d4d06e4b46ef","type":"text","text":"### Gestalt Psychology\n\nEmphasis on holistic perception - “the whole is more than the sum of its parts”.\n\n\n-Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Köhler, Kurt Koffka","x":1870,"y":2710,"width":329,"height":220},
		{"id":"674cb9a673235f10","type":"text","text":"### Behaviorism \n\nFocus on **observable behavior** and learning through conditioning; rejected introspection.\n\n-John B. Watson, B.F. Skinner, Ivan Pavlov","x":1410,"y":2710,"width":329,"height":220},
		{"id":"bcbbac7fc1df106f","type":"text","text":"### Sigmund Freud and Psychoanalysis\nemphasizing unconscious processes, childhood experiences, and drives\n","x":1170,"y":2480,"width":329,"height":180},
		{"id":"7f0ec2d1d4224ff7","type":"text","text":"# Charles Darwin \n- The brain as the seat of all behavior. \n- Animals could serve as models of human behavior. \n\n\n","x":830,"y":1780,"width":349,"height":180},
		{"id":"7e5551dd3e5f63ba","type":"text","text":"The rise of ethology, \n- Investigation of behavior of animals in their natural settings\n- Later to experimental psychology, the study of human and animal behavior under controlled conditions.","x":829,"y":2040,"width":351,"height":223},
		{"id":"517a2c77296b4f98","type":"text","text":"Formulation of the **Law of Specific Nerve Energies.**\n\n\"The nature of a sensation is determined by the nerve that is stimulated, not the stimulus itself.\"\n\n-Johannes Müller 1830s.","x":570,"y":-280,"width":250,"height":285},
		{"id":"2f971ef346094e41","type":"text","text":"Confirmation of the **electrical nature of the nerve signal.**\n\n-Emil du Bois-Reymond, 1840s","x":695,"y":54,"width":250,"height":185},
		{"id":"9d9959cbfabb4c08","type":"text","text":"#### Detailed, accurate descriptions of nerve cells\n\n-Italian Camillo Golgi and the Spaniard Santiago Ramón y Cajal\n\n","x":110,"y":640,"width":329,"height":143},
		{"id":"7e920b778854636d","type":"text","text":"- Golgi's Neurons staining method w/ silver salts revealed structures.","x":110,"y":900,"width":329,"height":95},
		{"id":"684098914f3a9f98","type":"text","text":"Ramón y Cajal discovered that nervous tissue is NOT a syncytium, BUT a network of discrete cells. ","x":110,"y":1040,"width":329,"height":100},
		{"id":"f7ed04cdeaa66b90","type":"text","text":"Development of **the neuron doctrine**\n-> The principle that individual neurons are the elementary building blocks and signaling elements of the nervous system.","x":110,"y":1200,"width":329,"height":180},
		{"id":"2118de3d4094cf61","type":"text","text":"### Existence of synapses.\n-Electron Microscopy Study by Sanford Palay","x":2271,"y":1225,"width":329,"height":130},
		{"id":"dcd470012ee94f14","type":"text","text":"#### \"Nerves convey fluid secreted by the brain and spinal cord to the body’s periphery.\" \n\n-2nd century Greek physician Galen ","x":-1010,"y":873,"width":329,"height":213},
		{"id":"01d31e095da53556","type":"text","text":"- **Hughlings Jackson** ->  Showed that different motor and sensory functions could be traced to specific parts of the cerebral cortex.\n","x":190,"y":5293,"width":329,"height":151},
		{"id":"f0767f25d6242b60","type":"text","text":"### Since the invention of fMRI, PET\n- Studies on healthy people\n-  Reading, Speaking and Thinking activate different brain areas each.","x":3890,"y":6077,"width":440,"height":150},
		{"id":"10d8829b316d41a7","type":"text","text":"Measurement of the **speed of nerve conduction** in **1850** (roughly **mid-19th century**).\n\n-Hermann von Helmholtz, 1850s","x":820,"y":280,"width":250,"height":225},
		{"id":"f7c07c4f8f45c17d","type":"text","text":"Luigi Galvani discovered that muscle and nerve cells produce electricity.","x":47,"y":101,"width":329,"height":90},
		{"id":"b7fc09874ff1be9a","type":"text","text":"Kant believed that our perception of the world is determined by inherent features of our mind or brain. \n\n-Immanuel Kant's major works on perception and the structure of the mind—especially the **Critique of Pure Reason** —were published in **1781** (revised 1787)","x":84,"y":-1020,"width":292,"height":320},
		{"id":"5febb43574d801ae","type":"text","text":"The brain is initially a blank slate (tabula rasa) that is later filled by sensory experience.","x":84,"y":-580,"width":292,"height":110},
		{"id":"478beaaf103dc3f6","type":"text","text":"René Descartes distinguished body\nand mind. \nIn Descartes’ dualistic view, \n- The brain mediates perception, motor acts, memory, appetites, and passions—everything that can be found in the lower animals. \n- But the mind—the higher mental functions, the conscious experience characteristic of human behavior—is  represented in the soul, a spiritual entity that communicates with the machinery of the brain by means of the pineal gland.\n","x":-450,"y":-980,"width":329,"height":480},
		{"id":"80d5f1741accad70","type":"text","text":"1800s","x":250,"y":4280,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"9f91998babd13991","type":"text","text":"1900s","x":1310,"y":4280,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c7aafe89edae3319","type":"text","text":"### Flaws\n- No clinical evidence supporting the theory.\n- Theories were based on whims rather than evidence.\n- Invention of a pseudoscience.","x":-1140,"y":6240,"width":329,"height":220},
		{"id":"6db33bf969ed5888","type":"text","text":"Brodmann distinguished 52 anatomically and functionally distinct areas in the human cerebral cortex.\n- Based on the shapes of cells and variations in their layered arrangement. \n\n-Broadmann's Classification of Cortical Areas (1909)","x":2660,"y":5881,"width":492,"height":218},
		{"id":"33442df7f48e3d8e","type":"text","text":"Skeptic of Broadmann's model, Lashley conducted studies of effects of various brain lesions on the ability of rats to learn to run a maze.\n\nFrom these studies Lashley concluded that the severity of a learning defect depended on the size of the lesion, not on its precise location.\n\nDisillusioned, Lashley—and after him many other psychologists—concluded that **learning and other higher mental functions have no special locus in the brain** and consequently cannot be attributed to specific collections of neurons.\n\n#### Lashley’s theory of mass action","x":2686,"y":7041,"width":440,"height":459},
		{"id":"a0e1ab9d598abbb7","type":"text","text":"Wernicke proposed -\n- **Most basic mental functions**, (simple perceptual and motor activities) are mediated by neurons in discrete local areas of the cortex. \n- **More complex cognitive functions**, result from interconnections between several functional sites.\n\nIn **placing the principle of localized function within a connectionist framework**, Wernicke realized that **different components of a single behavior are likely to be processed in several regions of the brain.** -> **distributed processing.** ","x":1810,"y":6580,"width":485,"height":340,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"8efaaeba93d65d9a","type":"text","text":"Hirsch and colleagues differentiated processing sites of native language from second language in Broca's Area.\nNo such differentiation is seen in Wernicke's Area.\n\n# Early bilingual\n\n![[image-93.png]]\n# Late bilingual\n\n![[image-92.png]]","x":3840,"y":6317,"width":540,"height":1110},
		{"id":"d765e2e605b9e3e0","type":"text","text":"Region of Broca’s area concerned with second\nlanguage becomes established and increases in activity only when an individual learns a second language that is “natural,” that is, one that shares the **universal grammar.** \n\nIf the second language is an artificial language,\na language that violates the rules of universal grammar, activity in Broca’s area does not increase.","x":4520,"y":6707,"width":440,"height":300},
		{"id":"6424665e976773cf","type":"text","text":"- First, the cognitive processing for language occurs in the left hemisphere and is independent of pathways that process the sensory and motor modalities used in language.\n- Second, fully functional auditory and motor systems are not necessary conditions for the emergence and operation of language capabilities in the left hemisphere.\n- Third, spoken language represents only one of a family of language skills mediated by the left hemisphere.","x":4520,"y":7087,"width":440,"height":340},
		{"id":"a25b22b1600fc973","type":"text","text":"eg. ASL shares the same universal grammar (Studies in the deaf)","x":5040,"y":6827,"width":440,"height":61},
		{"id":"eedbf4d81d91cf32","type":"text","text":"Because language is a uniquely human capability, Charles Darwin suggested that **the acquisition of language is an inborn instinct comparable to that for upright posture.**\nIn 1960 the linguist Noam Chomsky elaborated on Darwin’s notion. He proposed that children acquire a language so easily and naturally because humans, unlike other primates, have the innate capability of generalizing to a complete and coherent language from a limited sample of sentences.\n\n#### A Universal grammar","x":4520,"y":6228,"width":440,"height":399},
		{"id":"7ff29fe2a2835547","type":"text","text":"# Broca's Work (1824-1880)","x":1350,"y":5340,"width":420,"height":57},
		{"id":"3c1c413d0b8b3d98","type":"text","text":"Modern Neuroscience was influenced by Phrenology, a pseudoscience","x":1050,"y":5497,"width":304,"height":97,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"3e4fa0297117ce0e","type":"text","text":"Electrical stimulation of specific areas in the precentral gyrus led to movements in specific parts of the body in dogs.\n- Movements of limb of one side appeared from the motor cortex of the other side\n\n-Gustav Fritsch and Eduard Hitzig, 1870 ","x":1049,"y":5712,"width":305,"height":320},
		{"id":"aa9d08f5653c23f1","type":"text","text":"- The right hand, most used for writing and skilled movements is controlled by the left hemisphere\n","x":1049,"y":6122,"width":305,"height":125},
		{"id":"94199189b7691bc0","type":"text","text":"\nIn most people, therefore, the left hemisphere is regarded as dominant.","x":1049,"y":6327,"width":305,"height":100},
		{"id":"343a2d1a6768aac8","type":"text","text":"Lesion in the posterior region of the frontal lobe, (now called **Broca’s area of Motor Speech**) caused impairment in vocalisation of speech while comprehension was unaffected.","x":1435,"y":5747,"width":250,"height":230},
		{"id":"63a4a8c38dfdd088","type":"text","text":"This discovery led Broca\nto announce in 1864: “Nous parlons avec l’hémisphère\ngauche!” (We speak with the left hemisphere!)","x":1435,"y":6077,"width":250,"height":190},
		{"id":"8cd015826690a800","type":"text","text":"Many studies showed Lashley's technique of maze learning is unsuited to the search for local cortical functions because it involves so many motor and sensory capabilities. \nDeprived of one sensory capability, say\nvision, a rat can still learn to run a maze using touch or smell.","x":2686,"y":7600,"width":440,"height":208},
		{"id":"18abc3ac4286e163","type":"text","text":"Flourens systematically disproved Gall's theory by injuring specific sites of the brain.\n\nFlourens proposed,\nAny part of a cerebral hemisphere is able to perform all the hemisphere’s functions. Injury to any one area of the cerebral hemisphere should therefore affect all higher functions equally.\n\nCame to be k/a \"The holistic view of the brain\"","x":-320,"y":7040,"width":329,"height":380},
		{"id":"c528afb046483d78","type":"text","text":"Franz Joseph Gall's _naive_ approach. \n1. The brain is the organ of the mind and that all mental functions emanate from the brain. He rejected the idea that mind and body are separate entities. \n2. He also argued that the cerebral cortex did not function as a single organ but contained within it many organs, and that particular regions of the cerebral cortex control specific functions.","x":-1140,"y":4820,"width":329,"height":400},
		{"id":"ddfd8fdf7bba3fde","type":"text","text":"![[image-85.png]]\nAcc. to 19th century doctrine of phrenology, \n- Complex traits such as combativeness, spirituality, hope, and conscientiousness are controlled by specific areas in the brain.\n- Overuse hypertrophy and Disuse atrophy (of specific traits) gave rise to bumps (now, gyri) and ridges (now, sulci) on the overlying skull\n- Thus, they theorized that an individual’s character could be determined from the patterns on their skull.","x":-1140,"y":5300,"width":329,"height":860},
		{"id":"03739d3736f43a2d","type":"text","text":"Described another type of aphasia, \n\nA failure of comprehension related to an area posterior part of the cortex where the temporal lobe meets the parietal and occipital lobes (now called **Wernicke's Area of Sensory Speech**)\n\n-“The Symptom-Complex of Aphasia: A Psychological Study\non an Anatomical Basis.” by Karl Wernicke\n\nWERNICKE WAS 26 YEARS OLD!","x":1810,"y":5473,"width":485,"height":307},
		{"id":"959b31910b74a395","type":"text","text":"# Wernicke's Work (1848-1905)","x":1810,"y":5340,"width":485,"height":57},
		{"id":"983629f1bc4d6e73","type":"text","text":"![[image-88.png]]\nFig.1-4 Eric R. Kandel's Principles of Neural Science","x":1560,"y":4820,"width":485,"height":340},
		{"id":"da1072fa6e58ebdb","type":"text","text":"- Influenced by Gall's Work\n- Took the phrenological approach BUT used clinical evidence\n","x":1420,"y":5485,"width":280,"height":120},
		{"id":"0f469e92e3ebeea4","type":"text","text":"1920s","x":1890,"y":4280,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"fdf2a38add54f8ae","type":"text","text":"1950s","x":2475,"y":4280,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e9acbd6a7b1880a0","type":"text","text":"2nd Century","x":-930,"y":4280,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3848bea1ed07b2be","type":"text","text":"1700s","x":-335,"y":4280,"width":250,"height":60}
	],
